#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-02.png", width: 526.5pt), 34.5pt, 67.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(36pt, 59.4pt, "Appl. Sci. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(69.96pt, 59.4pt, "2023")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(86.04pt, 59.4pt, ",")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(87.96pt, 59.4pt, " 13")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(98.04pt, 59.4pt, ", x FOR PEER REVIEW ")
  #line-at(532.32pt, 59.4pt, "2 of 19 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.44pt, 102.24pt, "literature contains several references for optimal placement and location of PRVs along ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 115.08pt, "the network [15,16]. The PRVs are placed in the WDN working under different schemes: ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 127.92pt, "fixed outlet (FO), time-controlled (TC), flow modulation (FM) and critical point control ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 140.64pt, "(CPC) [17], with the main objective of reducing excess pressure which generates water ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 153.48pt, "leakage and water losses [18]. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 166.32pt, "FO is the simplest and most common scheme and only requires the installation of the ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 179.16pt, "PRV. It is suitable mainly for areas with uniform supply features and low headloss. The ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 192pt, "main limitation of this type of control is the inability to adapt to a variable demand pa")
  #line-at(536.16pt, 192pt, "Ĵ")
  #line-at(542.4pt, 192pt, "ern, ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 204.72pt, "common of areas with seasonal demand. The rest of the schemes, TC, FM and CPC, bring ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 217.56pt, "additional benefits to the operation of the network, but their installation is more complex, ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 230.4pt, "costly, requires additional equipment and the operator workforce must be well trained to ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 243.24pt, "support the installation, operation and maintenance [19].  ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 256.08pt, "TC consists of se")
  #line-at(261.48pt, 256.08pt, "Ĵ")
  #line-at(267.72pt, 256.08pt, "ing different values of pressure at the outlet of the PRV according ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 268.8pt, "to the time of the day. TC might be achieved by the addition of hydromechanical parts to ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 281.64pt, "the control trim of the PRV or by the utilization of electronic controllers. The most com-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 294.48pt, "mon application is double set-point, where pressure during the minimum consumption ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 307.32pt, "period, usually night time, is lowered to reduce leakage and kept at a higher value during ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 320.16pt, "peak demand time to satisfy customer demand. Despite this method being widely em-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 332.88pt, "ployed, the literature does not contain too many references about how to select the right ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 345.72pt, "se")
  #line-at(175.44pt, 345.72pt, "Ĵ")
  #line-at(181.68pt, 345.72pt, "ings of the valve. Ulanicki et al. [20] developed a model to calculate time schedules at ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 358.56pt, "the outlet of the PRV utilizing a nonlinear programming problem. The use of a genetic ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 371.4pt, "algorithm for this purpose was later introduced by Nicolini et al. [21]. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 384.24pt, "FM pressure-controlled systems rely on a flow signal generated by a flow meter, usu-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 396.96pt, "ally a pulse output or a 4–20 mA signal [22]. This system dynamically adjusts the pressure ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 409.8pt, "at the outlet of the PRV according to the flow, following a set-point table introduced by ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 422.64pt, "the user. While FM has remarkable benefits, such as being an effective method to address ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 435.48pt, "background leakage [23] and the possibility to adapt to real time demand [24], it also en-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 448.32pt, "counters some inconveniences, mainly from an operational point of view. These re-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 461.04pt, "strictions relate to the unavailability of a working flow meter at the installation site: some-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 473.88pt, "times it is not an option to install a flow meter due to lack of budget or because there is ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 486.72pt, "not enough space at the installation site. Another inconvenience appears when the flow ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 499.56pt, "meter malfunctions or when there are issues for transmi")
  #line-at(410.52pt, 499.56pt, "Ĵ")
  #line-at(416.76pt, 499.56pt, "ing the signal, common in cham-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 512.4pt, "bers and places prone to floodings. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 525.12pt, "CPC is the most sophisticated control scheme and aims to deliver a target pressure ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 537.96pt, "in the critical point or critical points of the area supplied by the PRV. The critical point can ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 550.8pt, "be defined as the first point in the WDN where the pressure falls below the minimum ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 563.64pt, "desired pressure [25]. Therefore, keeping the pressure in this point just above the mini-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 576.48pt, "mum will secure the right level of service in the rest of the pressure-managed area. The ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 589.32pt, "development of this control method has run in parallel to the development of communi-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 602.04pt, "cation technologies which allow linking the PRV with the critical point of the system [26]. ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 614.88pt, "The control algorithms for CPC might be closed-loop [27], where there is real-time com-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 627.72pt, "munication between the critical point and the PRV, or open-loop. The open-loop model is ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 640.56pt, "similar to the FM, with the difference that the model is built to deliver a fixed pressure in ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 653.4pt, "the critical point of the area [5]. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 666.12pt, "The benefits obtained by each of the methods are discussed in the literature, defining ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 678.96pt, "advantages and disadvantages for each of them [28–30]. All these papers conclude that ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 691.8pt, "dynamic models are the most convenient under variable demand conditions. However, ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 704.64pt, "the cost of implementation, operation and maintenance must be assessed beforehand. Un-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 717.48pt, "fortunately, most of the works existing in the literature are only experimental, with just a ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 730.2pt, "few references of results in real WDN [31]. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 743.04pt, "The aim of this work is to obtain an open-loop statistical model based on data from a ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 755.88pt, "time series of pressures to keep a fixed pressure at the critical point of the WDN. The ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 768.72pt, "SARIMA model will be used for this purpose. The main advantage of this model for pres-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 781.56pt, "sure management (PM) is that, unlike other CPC methods, it does not need a flow meter ")
]

