  #line-at(187.68pt, 347.28pt, "The new model proposed in this paper will target the same critical point pressure ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 360.12pt, "data. These values aim to reduce the pressure to the minimum possible when the demand ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 372.96pt, "is low to reduce leakage rates and increase during high consumption period to secure the ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 385.68pt, "right level of service to the customers. ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.44pt, 410.52pt, "2.3. Optimization Model ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(187.68pt, 426.36pt, "The seasonal autoregressive integrated moving average (SARIMA) model is em-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 439.2pt, "ployed specifically for data series with a seasonal component. This model is equivalent to ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 452.04pt, "the autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA) model, with the exception of the ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 464.76pt, "S component for the seasonal factor. The model is obtained by combining autoregressive ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 477.6pt, "analysis and moving averages of the values and errors due to the randomness of the time ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 490.44pt, "series. In this way, a value of the time series considered is related or is due to the correla-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 503.28pt, "tion of the previous values and with the moving averages of the statistical residuals of the ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 516.12pt, "time series considered to obtain the model. Once the model is adjusted, forecasts are made ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 528.96pt, "for the modeled series of values. ")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 541.68pt, "Data modeling versus time is obtained following a Box–Jenkins approach consisting ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 554.52pt, "of four stages: model identification, parameter estimation, model diagnosis and forecast ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 567.36pt, "verification. The identification is performed with the autocorrelation function (")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(516.72pt, 567.36pt, "ACF")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(536.16pt, 567.36pt, ") and ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 580.2pt, "partial autocorrelation function (")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(311.16pt, 580.2pt, "PACF")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(336pt, 580.2pt, ") plots. However, the stationarity of the time series ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 593.04pt, "must also be checked, as it is a fundamental criterion for applying the proposed approach.")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 605.76pt, "The Dickey–Fuller technique (1979) [50] is one of the most widely used methods to ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 618.6pt, "demonstrate the stationarity of the series. It is based on a hypothesis test where the null ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 631.44pt, "hypothesis indicates that the series is not stationary and has unit roots, and the alternative ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 644.28pt, "hypothesis indicates that the series is stationary and has no unit roots.")
  #line-at(187.68pt, 657.12pt, "The autocorrelation function (")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(323.52pt, 657.12pt, "ACF")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(342.96pt, 657.12pt, ") represents the correlation of each value of the ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 669.84pt, "series considered with the lagged values at different lengths. The values to be estimated ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 682.68pt, "are those above the confidence limits. The autocorrelation is calculated using the follow-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 695.52pt, "ing expression: ")
  #line-at(292.44pt, 715.08pt, "𝐴𝐶𝐹(𝑘) 𝑜𝐴𝐶(𝑘) = ")
  #line-at(375.12pt, 707.4pt, "𝐶𝑜𝑣(𝑦")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(401.52pt, 709.44pt, "௧")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(405.36pt, 707.4pt, ", 𝑦")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(414.24pt, 709.44pt, "௧ି௞")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(427.56pt, 707.04pt, ")")
  #line-at(386.28pt, 721.8pt, "𝑉𝑎𝑟(𝑦")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(412.56pt, 723.84pt, "௧")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.28pt, 721.44pt, ")")
  #line-at(547.56pt, 714.84pt, "(1)")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 733.8pt, "where ")
  #line-at(198.6pt, 733.8pt, "𝐶𝑜𝑣")
  #line-at(215.64pt, 733.8pt, " is a first lag correlation in the relationship of each of the values of the time ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 746.64pt, "series, at an instant ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(255.6pt, 746.64pt, "t")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(258.96pt, 746.64pt, ", with the values measured at the previous instant, ")
  #set text(font: "Arial", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(489.84pt, 746.64pt, "t-k")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(501pt, 746.64pt, " and without ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 759.48pt, "taking into account the intermediate values when the lags are greater. This function, in ")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 772.32pt, "ARIMA models, indicates the order (q) of the moving averages. The partial autocorrela-")
  #line-at(166.44pt, 785.04pt, "tion function (PACF) is used to find the partial correlation between the separated ")
